# schema-to-library

A code generator: a JSON Schema / OpenAPI document in, **TypeScript source text** for a
validation library out. Five targets — Zod, Valibot, Effect Schema, TypeBox, ArkType —
each written in that library's own idiom. Generators build strings; nothing is evaluated
at generation time, so a mistake is only caught by the fixtures and by type-checking the
generated files against the real library.

## Layout

| Path | What it is |
| --- | --- |
| `packages/schema-to-library` | the published npm package: parser, generators, five CLIs |
| `packages/schema-to-library/src/generator/<lib>` | one directory per target library |
| `fixtures/<lib>/<case>/{input.json,output.ts}` | golden files — `output.ts` is generated and committed |
| `fixtures/validation`, `fixtures/elysia` | runtime tests: the emitted schemas must actually accept/reject data |
| `apps/generated` | end-to-end smoke: runs the built CLIs over `apps/generated/schema/*` and type-checks the result |

`README.md` at the repository root is a symlink to `@packages/schema-to-library/README.md`;
there is one README, and it is what npm shows. It is deliberately minimal — what the tool
is, how to install it, the five commands, one worked example — and it stays that way.

## Commands

The toolchain is [Vite+](https://viteplus.dev/) (`vp`), not npm scripts. `-r` is recursive
over the workspace, `pkg#task` targets one workspace.

```bash
vp run -r check                    # format + lint + typecheck, every workspace
vp fmt                             # format only (vp fmt --check in CI)
vp run schema-to-library#build     # build dist/ — fixtures and apps/ import the built package
vp run @fixtures/zod#generate      # regenerate one library's fixtures (needs a fresh build)
vp test                            # fixture assertions, from the root config
vp run schema-to-library#test      # the library's own unit suite
vp run schema-to-library#coverage  # 80% statements/branches/functions/lines, enforced
vp run -r typecheck                # generated fixtures vs. the real target libraries
vp run lint                        # Markdown, terminology, spelling, secrets, links
```

## Non-negotiables

- **Never hand-edit generated files** — `fixtures/*/*/output.ts` and
  `apps/generated/generated/**`. Change the generator, rebuild, regenerate. A generated
  file edited by hand is a lie that survives until the next `generate` run.
- **The fixture diff is the review artifact.** After touching a generator, rebuild,
  regenerate, and read the `output.ts` diff before claiming the change works.
- **New emitted API must type-check against the real library** (`vp run -r typecheck`).
  Guessing at an API that the pinned version does not have is the most common failure
  here — see `@.cursor/rules/target-libraries.mdc`.
- **Document behaviour where it lives, not in the README.** A new `x-*` extension is
  documented by the doc comment on its type in `src/parser/x-ext/` and by the fixture that
  exercises it. Only installation, the commands and the one example belong in the README.
- **No new runtime dependencies** in `packages/schema-to-library` without being asked.
- **Work on a branch and land it through a pull request** — a version bump on `main`
  publishes to npm. See `@.cursor/rules/pull-requests.mdc`.
